1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,134
Senior Angular interview preparation, lesson 7.

2
00:00:03,134 --> 00:00:06,792
Angular Performance: Zoneless Rendering and Resource Lifetimes.

3
00:00:09,000 --> 00:00:10,869
Correct rendering comes before optimization

4
00:00:11,994 --> 00:00:13,863
Welcome to the performance lesson.

5
00:00:13,863 --> 00:00:17,601
The question is a copy button that depends on zone.js,

6
00:00:17,601 --> 00:00:22,461
measures its width through a zone event, and resets its status too early

7
00:00:22,461 --> 00:00:23,583
after repeated clicks.

8
00:00:23,583 --> 00:00:25,826
We will first understand rendering notifications,

9
00:00:25,826 --> 00:00:28,443
then fix state, timing, layout, error feedback,

10
00:00:28,443 --> 00:00:29,565
and resource cleanup.

11
00:00:30,940 --> 00:00:33,353
Correct rendering comes before optimization · 2

12
00:00:34,478 --> 00:00:38,037
Performance is not just making a component execute fewer times.

13
00:00:38,037 --> 00:00:42,308
A fast component that fails to show the current state is incorrect.

14
00:00:42,308 --> 00:00:45,867
Start by tracing the operation: the click begins a clipboard

15
00:00:45,867 --> 00:00:48,714
request, the promise settles, the copied state changes,

16
00:00:48,714 --> 00:00:52,273
a timer later resets it, and the view must reflect

17
00:00:52,273 --> 00:00:52,985
both transitions.

18
00:00:52,985 --> 00:00:56,545
Ask which API tells Angular that each transition needs rendering.

19
00:00:57,920 --> 00:01:00,499
Correct rendering comes before optimization · 3

20
00:01:01,624 --> 00:01:04,689
A senior answer connects scheduling to observable behavior.

21
00:01:04,689 --> 00:01:08,138
It also questions whether the JavaScript work is needed.

22
00:01:08,138 --> 00:01:11,970
Measuring a button can be more complex and less reliable

23
00:01:11,970 --> 00:01:15,419
than allowing CSS to reserve space for both labels.

24
00:01:15,419 --> 00:01:20,785
Removing an unnecessary layout read improves performance and simplifies correctness at the same time.

25
00:01:22,160 --> 00:01:24,146
Zone-based and zoneless change detection

26
00:01:25,271 --> 00:01:28,448
Zone-based applications use patched asynchronous activity as a

27
00:01:28,448 --> 00:01:31,228
broad indication that something may have changed.

28
00:01:31,228 --> 00:01:35,995
This can make plain fields appear to update after timers or promises.

29
00:01:35,995 --> 00:01:39,966
It is not a universal guarantee: work outside the zone,

30
00:01:39,966 --> 00:01:44,335
unpatched APIs, and native asynchronous behavior can still require explicit notification.

31
00:01:44,335 --> 00:01:48,705
Avoid teaching that every promise completion automatically fixes every state update.

32
00:01:50,080 --> 00:01:52,567
Zone-based and zoneless change detection · 2

33
00:01:53,692 --> 00:01:55,944
Zoneless rendering relies on Angular notifications.

34
00:01:55,944 --> 00:01:59,322
Signals read by a template notify when they change.

35
00:01:59,322 --> 00:02:02,324
Bound template and host listeners, component setInput, markForCheck,

36
00:02:02,324 --> 00:02:05,327
and the async pipe are other important paths.

37
00:02:05,327 --> 00:02:10,206
A plain field assignment in an arbitrary asynchronous callback is not a notification.

38
00:02:10,206 --> 00:02:13,585
Neither is a raw timer merely because it ran.

39
00:02:14,960 --> 00:02:17,537
Zone-based and zoneless change detection · 3

40
00:02:18,663 --> 00:02:21,721
OnPush is compatible with this model when the

41
00:02:21,721 --> 00:02:24,398
component observes its inputs and notifications correctly.

42
00:02:24,398 --> 00:02:27,456
It does not make all mutable state observable.

43
00:02:27,456 --> 00:02:32,045
Some dynamic-host library components require additional care for children with different assumptions.

44
00:02:32,045 --> 00:02:35,486
Test the behavior in a zoneless consumer rather than

45
00:02:35,486 --> 00:02:38,545
treating an OnPush annotation as proof of compatibility.

46
00:02:39,919 --> 00:02:41,585
Async boundaries and view notifications

47
00:02:42,711 --> 00:02:46,709
A click listener can schedule a render before its awaited work finishes.

48
00:02:46,709 --> 00:02:49,709
If the handler later writes a plain copied field,

49
00:02:49,709 --> 00:02:53,041
that earlier render does not promise another check after the

50
00:02:53,041 --> 00:02:53,374
write.

51
00:02:53,374 --> 00:02:56,040
An unrelated event may make the label appear,

52
00:02:56,040 --> 00:02:59,039
which creates the misleading impression that the code usually

53
00:02:59,039 --> 00:02:59,372
works.

54
00:02:59,372 --> 00:03:03,705
Tests should isolate the completion and observe whether it schedules its own update.

55
00:03:05,080 --> 00:03:07,674
Async boundaries and view notifications · 2

56
00:03:08,799 --> 00:03:12,236
Use a signal for state consumed by the template

57
00:03:12,236 --> 00:03:15,291
or explicitly mark a suitable view for checking.

58
00:03:15,291 --> 00:03:19,111
For observable data, the async pipe connects emissions to rendering.

59
00:03:19,111 --> 00:03:22,930
For third-party callbacks, adapt data at the boundary instead of

60
00:03:22,930 --> 00:03:26,368
requiring product teams to click somewhere else to refresh.

61
00:03:26,368 --> 00:03:29,423
NgZone.run is not itself a zoneless notification mechanism,

62
00:03:29,423 --> 00:03:33,243
although retaining run and runOutsideAngular can still matter for zone-based

63
00:03:33,243 --> 00:03:33,625
consumers.

64
00:03:35,000 --> 00:03:37,975
Async boundaries and view notifications · 3

65
00:03:39,100 --> 00:03:42,925
Server rendering has a related but separate stability contract.

66
00:03:42,925 --> 00:03:47,600
Pending asynchronous work that must complete before serialization may need PendingTasks.

67
00:03:47,600 --> 00:03:51,425
A render notification tells Angular that a view changed;

68
00:03:51,425 --> 00:03:54,825
it does not automatically describe every server-side task.

69
00:03:54,825 --> 00:03:59,925
Keep browser clipboard actions out of server execution and document environment-dependent APIs.

70
00:04:01,300 --> 00:04:04,011
Use render hooks for DOM timing

71
00:04:05,136 --> 00:04:09,738
NgZone onStable and related observables do not emit in a zoneless application.

72
00:04:09,738 --> 00:04:14,341
They are the wrong boundary for deciding that a view is ready.

73
00:04:14,341 --> 00:04:18,177
Use the supported render callbacks when a DOM operation truly

74
00:04:18,177 --> 00:04:22,012
depends on the rendered view. afterNextRender handles a one-time operation;

75
00:04:22,012 --> 00:04:25,464
a post-render effect can react to dependencies after rendering.

76
00:04:26,839 --> 00:04:30,130
Use render hooks for DOM timing · 2

77
00:04:31,255 --> 00:04:35,119
Separate geometry reads from style writes when doing coordinated DOM work.

78
00:04:35,119 --> 00:04:38,633
A write followed by a read can force layout repeatedly.

79
00:04:38,633 --> 00:04:40,741
Render phases help organize that work,

80
00:04:40,741 --> 00:04:43,552
but the best improvement can be removing the

81
00:04:43,552 --> 00:04:44,255
measurement entirely.

82
00:04:44,255 --> 00:04:47,066
Ask what the measurement is trying to guarantee,

83
00:04:47,066 --> 00:04:49,525
whether it survives translation and font loading,

84
00:04:49,525 --> 00:04:51,984
and whether CSS already expresses the requirement.

85
00:04:53,360 --> 00:04:56,255
Use render hooks for DOM timing · 3

86
00:04:57,380 --> 00:04:59,845
Render callbacks do not execute during server rendering.

87
00:04:59,845 --> 00:05:03,542
That is useful for browser-only DOM work, but it also means the

88
00:05:03,542 --> 00:05:07,240
initial server output should not depend on a callback that never ran.

89
00:05:07,240 --> 00:05:09,705
Give the component a sensible CSS-first state and

90
00:05:09,705 --> 00:05:12,170
use browser work as an enhancement where necessary.

91
00:05:12,170 --> 00:05:16,484
Test the measured result in a real browser if you make a layout claim.

92
00:05:17,860 --> 00:05:19,999
Prefer a layout that handles both labels

93
00:05:21,124 --> 00:05:24,180
Locking a button to the width of Copy does not

94
00:05:24,180 --> 00:05:26,931
stop the longer label Copied from making it grow.

95
00:05:26,931 --> 00:05:29,070
Translations can reverse which label is longer,

96
00:05:29,070 --> 00:05:31,515
and a loaded font can change both widths.

97
00:05:31,515 --> 00:05:33,654
Measuring once before those changes is fragile.

98
00:05:33,654 --> 00:05:36,099
Place both labels in the same grid cell

99
00:05:36,099 --> 00:05:38,545
so the intrinsic width accommodates the larger one.

100
00:05:39,920 --> 00:05:42,792
Prefer a layout that handles both labels · 2

101
00:05:43,916 --> 00:05:46,150
Hide the inactive label with visibility hidden.

102
00:05:46,150 --> 00:05:48,703
It continues to participate in layout but is

103
00:05:48,703 --> 00:05:51,256
removed from normal rendering and the accessibility tree.

104
00:05:51,256 --> 00:05:54,766
Display none would remove its contribution to width; opacity zero would

105
00:05:54,766 --> 00:05:58,276
leave it in the accessibility tree unless other measures were taken.

106
00:05:58,276 --> 00:06:02,424
The choice of hiding mechanism is part of both layout and naming behavior.

107
00:06:03,800 --> 00:06:07,007
Prefer a layout that handles both labels · 3

108
00:06:08,132 --> 00:06:10,982
Verify the button's bounding rectangle before and after

109
00:06:10,982 --> 00:06:13,476
the status change with representative translated labels.

110
00:06:13,476 --> 00:06:16,683
Check the accessible name contains the visible label only.

111
00:06:16,683 --> 00:06:21,316
This is a concrete example of performance work that removes a layout read,

112
00:06:21,316 --> 00:06:26,305
a style mutation, a zone subscription, and timing assumptions while improving the user experience.

113
00:06:27,680 --> 00:06:29,585
Timers and promises have resource lifetimes

114
00:06:30,710 --> 00:06:33,567
Every timer has an owner and an end condition.

115
00:06:33,567 --> 00:06:36,742
Starting a new reset timer without cancelling the previous one

116
00:06:36,742 --> 00:06:39,600
lets an earlier click reset a later success message.

117
00:06:39,600 --> 00:06:42,140
Clear the old timer when beginning the next

118
00:06:42,140 --> 00:06:44,362
operation and before installing a new one.

119
00:06:44,362 --> 00:06:46,902
Clear it on destruction so callbacks do not

120
00:06:46,902 --> 00:06:49,125
update a component that no longer exists.

121
00:06:50,500 --> 00:06:53,208
Timers and promises have resource lifetimes · 2

122
00:06:54,333 --> 00:06:56,026
Promises introduce another edge case.

123
00:06:56,026 --> 00:06:58,734
Two clipboard requests can finish out of order.

124
00:06:58,734 --> 00:07:01,782
A first request can settle after a second one,

125
00:07:01,782 --> 00:07:04,491
creating a stale announcement or another reset timer.

126
00:07:04,491 --> 00:07:06,861
A completion can also arrive after destruction.

127
00:07:06,861 --> 00:07:10,585
Clearing a timer alone does not cancel an already pending promise.

128
00:07:10,585 --> 00:07:13,294
Use a request sequence and a destroyed-state check

129
00:07:13,294 --> 00:07:15,664
when the UI must ignore stale completions.

130
00:07:17,040 --> 00:07:19,957
Timers and promises have resource lifetimes · 3

131
00:07:21,083 --> 00:07:25,093
The source correction improves the timer behavior for ordinary sequential completions,

132
00:07:25,093 --> 00:07:28,740
but it does not fully guard those overlapping promise cases.

133
00:07:28,740 --> 00:07:33,480
We will provide an additional hardened teaching example and verify the sequence guard.

134
00:07:33,480 --> 00:07:38,585
State the limitation rather than presenting the minimal correction as a complete concurrency solution.

135
00:07:39,960 --> 00:07:42,475
Errors and announcements are part of completion

136
00:07:43,600 --> 00:07:46,968
The clipboard API can reject because permission was denied,

137
00:07:46,968 --> 00:07:51,085
the environment is unsupported, or the page is not a secure

138
00:07:51,085 --> 00:07:51,459
context.

139
00:07:51,459 --> 00:07:53,705
Catch failures and provide useful feedback.

140
00:07:53,705 --> 00:07:56,699
Do not show Copied before the operation succeeds.

141
00:07:56,699 --> 00:08:01,565
Do not leave a rejected promise unhandled while the UI silently remains unchanged.

142
00:08:02,940 --> 00:08:06,327
Errors and announcements are part of completion · 2

143
00:08:07,452 --> 00:08:12,343
A changing button label is not a dependable status announcement for screen readers.

144
00:08:12,343 --> 00:08:16,483
A live announcer can communicate success or failure without moving focus.

145
00:08:16,483 --> 00:08:17,988
Success is ordinarily polite.

146
00:08:17,988 --> 00:08:22,127
An assertive failure announcement should be justified by urgency and interruption

147
00:08:22,127 --> 00:08:26,267
cost; it is not automatically required for every rejected clipboard request.

148
00:08:26,267 --> 00:08:28,525
Expose translatable messages to the application.

149
00:08:29,900 --> 00:08:33,314
Errors and announcements are part of completion · 3

150
00:08:34,439 --> 00:08:38,232
Keep the final state coherent when a later operation fails.

151
00:08:38,232 --> 00:08:42,405
Clear or replace stale success, avoid allowing an old completion to

152
00:08:42,405 --> 00:08:46,578
overwrite a newer failure, and keep the button operable for retry.

153
00:08:46,578 --> 00:08:49,992
Tests should cover success, failure, overlap, reset, and destruction.

154
00:08:49,992 --> 00:08:54,545
These are correctness cases that prevent seemingly random performance or rendering reports.

155
00:08:55,920 --> 00:08:58,630
Test notifications instead of forcing every render

156
00:08:59,755 --> 00:09:02,852
A test that manually runs detectChanges after an

157
00:09:02,852 --> 00:09:05,949
asynchronous plain-field assignment can hide the production defect.

158
00:09:05,949 --> 00:09:09,046
For notification-sensitive cases, use zoneless change detection and

159
00:09:09,046 --> 00:09:11,756
wait for the fixture to become stable.

160
00:09:11,756 --> 00:09:15,240
Complete a controlled promise, then assert the visible label

161
00:09:15,240 --> 00:09:18,725
without forcing a render that Angular did not schedule.

162
00:09:20,100 --> 00:09:23,259
Test notifications instead of forcing every render · 2

163
00:09:24,384 --> 00:09:27,894
Mock the clipboard boundary so success and failure are deterministic.

164
00:09:27,894 --> 00:09:31,404
Control pending requests independently to finish them out of order.

165
00:09:31,404 --> 00:09:34,914
Use a short configurable reset delay in the hardened teaching

166
00:09:34,914 --> 00:09:38,424
example to verify the timer path without a slow test.

167
00:09:38,424 --> 00:09:41,232
Destroy the fixture while a request is pending,

168
00:09:41,232 --> 00:09:44,742
then complete it and confirm no new timer or announcement

169
00:09:44,742 --> 00:09:45,445
is created.

170
00:09:46,820 --> 00:09:50,088
Test notifications instead of forcing every render · 3

171
00:09:51,213 --> 00:09:55,377
Real browser tests are necessary for intrinsic layout and accessible names.

172
00:09:55,377 --> 00:09:59,541
A DOM emulator cannot prove that the two labels have the

173
00:09:59,541 --> 00:10:03,326
same outer button width or that native focus behaves correctly.

174
00:10:03,326 --> 00:10:05,598
Compile-time validation remains useful for types,

175
00:10:05,598 --> 00:10:08,626
but use the appropriate runtime evidence for every

176
00:10:08,626 --> 00:10:09,005
claim.

177
00:10:10,380 --> 00:10:11,924
Audit a library systematically

178
00:10:13,049 --> 00:10:17,862
Search for zone lifecycle subscriptions, plain fields written by timers or promises,

179
00:10:17,862 --> 00:10:23,477
direct DOM measurements, subscriptions without cleanup, and effects that allocate resources without an inverse

180
00:10:23,477 --> 00:10:23,878
operation.

181
00:10:23,878 --> 00:10:29,494
These searches identify candidates; they do not prove that every match is a defect.

182
00:10:29,494 --> 00:10:33,505
Review each candidate according to its ownership and notification path.

183
00:10:34,880 --> 00:10:37,284
Audit a library systematically · 2

184
00:10:38,409 --> 00:10:43,617
Run representative consumers without zone.js and interact with components through their actual templates.

185
00:10:43,617 --> 00:10:47,224
Include asynchronous data, input changes, multiple instances, and destruction.

186
00:10:47,224 --> 00:10:49,628
Add regression tests where behavior fails.

187
00:10:49,628 --> 00:10:54,035
A debug check for unnotified binding changes can help find gaps,

188
00:10:54,035 --> 00:10:58,844
but do not fix failures by forcing global change detection after every

189
00:10:58,844 --> 00:10:59,245
callback.

190
00:11:00,620 --> 00:11:02,939
Audit a library systematically · 3

191
00:11:04,064 --> 00:11:06,769
Profile only after the behavior is correct.

192
00:11:06,769 --> 00:11:09,475
Look for repeated expensive derivations, layout thrashing,

193
00:11:09,475 --> 00:11:12,568
unnecessary allocations, and excessive renders using measured traces.

194
00:11:12,568 --> 00:11:15,274
A signal is not a performance certificate,

195
00:11:15,274 --> 00:11:18,753
and a faster benchmark that dropped needed updates is

196
00:11:18,753 --> 00:11:19,140
misleading.

197
00:11:19,140 --> 00:11:23,005
In the question walkthrough, explain both why the original fails

198
00:11:23,005 --> 00:11:26,485
and why the corrected mechanism supplies the missing notification.

